  "m1d_bool_rebuild": {
    "_note": "angr-op0dn.9.6 \u2014 sizing input for M1.d / angr-op0dn.9.4 (extend the per-Expression Z3 AST memo to the native Bool path). Same 11 fast-tier benches as the S1 query-class census. Counters added in symbolic/stats.rs (z3_bool_build, z3_bool_build_nodes, z3_bool_memo_reused_nodes, z3_bool_memoized_subtree); RustBV::to_z3_bool brackets each top-level conversion with the (z3_ast_cache_miss, z3_ast_memo_hit) pair, so built = Dmiss - Dmemo_hit is the Z3-node work the call actually did and reused = Dmemo_hit is what the BV memo already served. READ THE RIGHT RATIO: the Bool path's SHARE of AST work is large (66.7% of top-level builds, 88.2% of all constructed nodes), but a memo can only remove REPEAT conversions. A repeat of a compound-operand Bool node lands in z3_bool_memoized_subtree by construction (its operand subtrees memo-hit, so it builds <=1 node \u2014 the comparison itself). That bucket is 442 calls / 442-ish nodes = 6.5% of all constructed nodes, each a single hash-consed mk_eq. The other 5,564 nodes the Bool path builds are FIRST conversions, which no memo removes. The bead predicted this ('each rebuild is one hash-consed Z3 node once operands memo-hit').",
    "corpus_totals": {
      "z3_ast_build": 1523,
      "z3_bool_build": 1016,
      "z3_bool_build_nodes": 6006,
      "z3_bool_memo_reused_nodes": 739,
      "z3_bool_memoized_subtree": 442,
      "all_built_nodes": 6808
    },
    "bool_share_of_top_level_builds_pct": 66.7,
    "bool_share_of_built_nodes_pct": 88.2,
    "memo_addressable_nodes_pct": 6.5,
    "nodes_saved_per_addressable_call": 1,
    "gate": "KILL if the Bool-REBUILD share of z3_ast_build measures trivial (angr-op0dn.9.4 acceptance criteria)",
    "verdict": "KILL angr-op0dn.9.4. Memo-addressable node-build work is 6.5% of the corpus total and is one hash-consed comparison node per call; the ovqja.3 BV memo already absorbs every compound subtree on the Bool path (z3_bool_memo_reused_nodes=739). Not worth a widened memo slot / side table plus its context-guard + translate-reset + serde-skip obligations.",
